---
import Reading from '../../../layouts/Reading.astro';
import { commitments, facts, parts } from '../../../data/book';
---
<Reading
  title="Chasing Rainbows — Kevin Holler"
  description="Chasing Rainbows — a book Kevin Holler is writing about starting three companies, two of which failed."
  here="book"
  layout="spread"
>
  <div class="spread">
    <div class="spread-main">
      <h1>Chasing Rainbows</h1>
      <p class="stamp">A book I am writing. Unfinished, and said so on purpose.</p>

      <p class="lede">
        Building a startup is like chasing a rainbow. It is a journey full of
        promise and possibility, but the closer you get, the further it seems
        to move.
      </p>

      <p>
        A little over ten years have passed since I graduated college in
        Ireland. In that time I started three companies — two of them failed —
        worked with five startups at varying stages, and somehow ended up
        writing software, selling it, and supporting it, sometimes in the same
        week. The career path has been everything but linear, and the three
        years of my first company taught me more about myself, people, and
        business than any other period of my life. Despite the pain of
        shutting a business down, I have been crazy enough to attempt it
        again.
      </p>

      <p>
        That is the book. Not a playbook — there are enough of those, and the
        people who write them tend to have won. This is the other account:
        what it costs, what it teaches, and why anyone does it a second and a
        third time.
      </p>

      <h2>Why I am writing it</h2>
      <p>
        When my first company failed I wrote a post announcing the shutdown
        and detailing some of what had contributed to it. In Ireland at the
        time, failure was rarely spoken about openly. Having already lost
        everything, I figured I had nothing left to lose. I published it, shut
        the laptop, and went for a walk.
      </p>
      <p>
        What followed twenty-four hours later shocked me: a stream of job
        offers, interview requests from media, founders emailing to share the
        same experience. The thing I was most afraid to say out loud was the
        thing other people most needed to hear. The book is that post, given
        the length it deserved.
      </p>

      <h2>What it is held to</h2>
      <p>
        Four commitments, and they are the reason it is taking as long as it
        is. Any chapter that cannot meet all four gets cut.
      </p>
      <dl class="facts">
        {
          commitments.map((commitment) => (
            <>
              <dt>{commitment.name}</dt>
              <dd>{commitment.gloss}</dd>
            </>
          ))
        }
      </dl>

      <h2>Where it is up to</h2>
      <p>
        Honestly: unfinished. A work in progress described honestly is more
        interesting than one described vaguely, so the parts are listed with
        the state each is actually in rather than a percentage.
      </p>
      <ul class="entries" style="margin-top: 20px;">
        {
          parts.map((part) => (
            <li>
              <div class="row">
                <span class="name">{part.title}</span>
                <span class="years">{part.status}</span>
              </div>
              <p class="blurb">{part.summary}</p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>

      <h2>If you want to know when it lands</h2>
      <p>
        There is no list to join yet. Until there is,
        <a href="mailto:hello@kevinholler.com">email me</a> and I will remember
        — or read <a href="/new/writing/">the writing</a>, which is where the
        chapters get tested first.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="spread-side">
      <dl class="notes">
        {
          facts.map((fact) => (
            <>
              <dt>{fact.label}</dt>
              <dd>{fact.value}</dd>
            </>
          ))
        }
      </dl>
      <p class="coverage">
        <em>The essay this came from:</em>{' '}
        <a href="/new/writing/chasing-rainbows/">Chasing rainbows</a>, 2024
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Reading>
